\title{Escher Staircases in Commutative Algebra:\\Infinite Ideal Growth, Orientation, and Noetherianity}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 17, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study an \emph{Escher staircase}, defined to be a strictly increasing sequence of ideals in a commutative ring. Existence of such a staircase is equivalent to failure of Noetherianity. We construct one explicitly in the product ring of integer sequences: ideals of sequences supported in finite initial segments form a strict ascending chain whose intersection is the zero ideal. Successively generated variable ideals give an analogous chain in a polynomial ring with countably many variables. Finite-variable polynomial rings over fields and rings of $p$-adic integers admit no staircase because they are Noetherian. By contrast, requiring function values to be divisible by $2^n$ produces a strictly \emph{descending} chain. These results show that staircase existence restates the ascending chain condition rather than defining a new invariant. We clarify the automatic nature of the intersection condition, distinguish the phenomenon from Krull dimension, and outline requirements for a meaningful refined height.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Ascending chains of ideals encode algebraic growth. If
\[
 I_0\subseteq I_1\subseteq I_2\subseteq\cdots,
\]
then each stage may incorporate new generators or consequences. The Noetherian condition says that such growth eventually stops. This finiteness principle supports finite descriptions of polynomial equations and termination phenomena in symbolic algebra.

An Escher staircase is the apparently impossible architecture
\[
 I_0\subsetneq I_1\subsetneq I_2\subsetneq\cdots.
\]
Its common foundation may coincide with its initial rung, but there is no paradox: an ascending chain beginning at a least member always has that member as its intersection. The genuine content is endless strict ascent.

This paper proves that a commutative ring admits such a staircase exactly when it is not Noetherian. It then gives positive examples in the ring of integer sequences and in a polynomial ring with countably many variables, and negative examples among finite-variable polynomial rings and $p$-adic integers. We also correct a tempting construction: divisibility by increasing powers of $2$ strengthens a condition and therefore makes ideals smaller.

Throughout, rings are commutative with identity.

\section{Definitions and order-theoretic facts}

\begin{definition}
A sequence $(I_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ of ideals of a ring $R$ is \emph{strictly ascending} if $I_m\subsetneq I_n$ whenever $m<n$. An \emph{Escher staircase} in $R$ is such a sequence.
\end{definition}

It suffices to prove $I_n\subsetneq I_{n+1}$ for every $n$, since strict containment is transitive.

\begin{definition}
A ring satisfies the \emph{ascending chain condition} if every chain
\[
 J_0\subseteq J_1\subseteq J_2\subseteq\cdots
\]
is eventually constant. A commutative ring with this property is \emph{Noetherian}.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Intersection of an ascending chain]
If $I_0\subseteq I_1\subseteq I_2\subseteq\cdots$, then
\[
 \bigcap_{n\geq 0}I_n=I_0.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The intersection is contained in every term, hence in $I_0$. Conversely, monotonicity gives $I_0\subseteq I_n$ for every $n$, so every element of $I_0$ lies in the intersection.
\end{proof}

Thus an additional requirement that the first rung equal the intersection is redundant. Moreover, the intersection contains ring elements, not ideals. Saying that an ideal is an element of this intersection confuses two levels of structure. Requiring merely that zero belong to all rungs is also vacuous, since every ideal contains zero.

\section{Characterization by Noetherianity}

\begin{theorem}[Staircase Characterization]
A commutative ring $R$ admits an Escher staircase if and only if $R$ is not Noetherian.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $(I_n)$ is an Escher staircase, then it is an ascending chain that never stabilizes, so $R$ is not Noetherian.

Conversely, suppose $R$ is not Noetherian. There is an ascending chain $(J_n)$ that is not eventually constant. Choose an index $n_0$. Having chosen $n_k$, non-stabilization provides $n_{k+1}>n_k$ such that $J_{n_k}\subsetneq J_{n_{k+1}}$; otherwise all later ideals would equal $J_{n_k}$. Setting $I_k=J_{n_k}$ gives an infinite strictly ascending subsequence.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Staircase existence is precisely failure of the ascending chain condition; it is not an independent binary invariant.
\end{corollary}

The theorem can be used diagnostically in either direction. An explicit staircase certifies non-Noetherianity. A proof of Noetherianity excludes every possible staircase at once.

\section{The product ring of integer sequences}
Let
\[
 R=\mathbb Z^{\mathbb N}=\prod_{k\in\mathbb N}\mathbb Z
\]
with coordinatewise addition and multiplication. Write $a=(a_0,a_1,\ldots)$.

\begin{definition}[Initial-support ideals]
For $n\in\mathbb N$, set
\[
 S_n=\{a\in R: a_k=0\text{ for every }k\geq n\}.
\]
Thus $S_n$ consists of sequences supported inside $\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
Each $S_n$ is an ideal of $R$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The zero sequence lies in $S_n$. Sums of sequences vanishing from coordinate $n$ onward have the same property. If $a\in S_n$ and $c\in R$, then $(ca)_k=c_ka_k=0$ for every $k\geq n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Strict adjacent growth]
For every $n$, one has $S_n\subsetneq S_{n+1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Vanishing for all $k\geq n$ implies vanishing for all $k\geq n+1$. Let $e_n$ have value $1$ at coordinate $n$ and $0$ elsewhere. Then $e_n\in S_{n+1}$ but $e_n\notin S_n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Product-ring staircase]
The chain
\[
 S_0\subsetneq S_1\subsetneq S_2\subsetneq\cdots
\]
is an Escher staircase in $\mathbb Z^{\mathbb N}$, and
\[
 \bigcap_{n\geq0}S_n=S_0=\{0\}.
\]
Consequently $\mathbb Z^{\mathbb N}$ is not Noetherian.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Strictness follows from the preceding lemma. Since the condition defining $S_0$ forces every coordinate to vanish, $S_0=\{0\}$. The intersection formula follows from the ascending-chain lemma, and non-Noetherianity follows from the characterization theorem.
\end{proof}

This construction frees one coordinate at each stage. The vector $e_n$ is a finite certificate that the new rung is genuinely larger.

\section{Polynomial rings}
Let $k$ be a field and consider
\[
 P=k[x_0,x_1,x_2,\ldots].
\]
Each polynomial uses finitely many variables, although no finite list contains every available variable.

\begin{definition}
For $n\in\mathbb N$, let
\[
 J_n=(x_0,x_1,\ldots,x_{n-1}),
\]
where $J_0=(0)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Countable-variable staircase]
The sequence $(J_n)$ is an Escher staircase, and $\bigcap_nJ_n=(0)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every generator of $J_n$ is a generator of $J_{n+1}$, so $J_n\subseteq J_{n+1}$. The variable $x_n$ belongs to $J_{n+1}$. It does not belong to $J_n$: specialize $x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1}$ to zero while retaining $x_n$ as an indeterminate. Every element of $J_n$ maps to zero, whereas $x_n$ does not. Thus each inclusion is strict. Since $J_0=(0)$, the intersection formula follows from the ascending-chain lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-variable obstruction]
For every finite $d\geq0$, the polynomial ring $k[x_1,\ldots,x_d]$ has no Escher staircase.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A field is Noetherian. Hilbert's basis theorem says that a polynomial ring in one variable over a Noetherian ring is Noetherian. Iterating it $d$ times proves that $k[x_1,\ldots,x_d]$ is Noetherian. The Staircase Characterization Theorem excludes an Escher staircase.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Krull dimension]
The Krull dimension of $k[x_1,\ldots,x_d]$ is $d$, as reflected by the finite prime chain
\[
 (0)\subsetneq(x_1)\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq(x_1,\ldots,x_d).
\]
This is compatible with Noetherianity. Krull dimension measures chains of prime ideals, while the staircase condition asks for an infinite ascending chain of arbitrary ideals. Therefore assigning ``Escher height $d$'' to this ring conflates distinct notions unless height is redefined.
\end{remark}

\section{Power-divisibility ideals run downward}
Let $F=\mathbb Z^{\mathbb Z}$ be the ring of functions $f:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z$ under pointwise operations.

\begin{definition}
For $n\in\mathbb N$, define
\[
 D_n=\{f\in F:2^n\mid f(z)\text{ for every }z\in\mathbb Z\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
Every $D_n$ is an ideal of $F$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Pointwise, zero is divisible by $2^n$, sums of multiples of $2^n$ remain multiples, and arbitrary integer multiples preserve divisibility. Applying these observations at every input proves the ideal axioms.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict descent of divisibility rungs]
For every $n$,
\[
 D_{n+1}\subsetneq D_n.
\]
Hence $(D_n)$ is strictly descending and cannot be an Escher staircase.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every multiple of $2^{n+1}$ is a multiple of $2^n$, proving containment. The constant function $c_n(z)=2^n$ belongs to $D_n$, but does not belong to $D_{n+1}$. Thus containment is strict.
\end{proof}

The same argument applies in function subrings containing the relevant constants, including an appropriate ring of integer-valued polynomials. Restricting the ambient functions does not reverse divisibility.

\begin{proposition}
The descending chain has zero intersection:
\[
 \bigcap_{n\geq0}D_n=\{0\}.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If $f$ lies in every $D_n$, then each integer $f(z)$ is divisible by every power of $2$. The only such integer is zero, so $f$ is the zero function.
\end{proof}

A zero intersection does not determine orientation. Adding generators enlarges ideals; increasing divisibility or vanishing requirements generally shrinks them.

\section{The $p$-adic integers}
Fix a prime $p$. The ring $\mathbb Z_p$ of $p$-adic integers is a discrete valuation ring with uniformizer $p$.

\begin{theorem}[$p$-adic obstruction]
The ring $\mathbb Z_p$ has no Escher staircase.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every discrete valuation ring is a principal ideal domain and hence Noetherian. The characterization theorem therefore rules out an Escher staircase. Concretely, the nonzero ideals have the descending form
\[
 \mathbb Z_p\supsetneq(p)\supsetneq(p^2)\supsetneq\cdots.
\]
An ascending chain moves toward smaller exponents and therefore stabilizes after finitely many strict steps.
\end{proof}

This example separates topological infinitude from algebraic non-Noetherianity. Infinite $p$-adic expansions do not create endless ascending ideal growth.

\section{Computational demonstrations}
Finite models are useful for exposing witnesses and auditing inclusion direction, even though they do not replace the general arguments.

\begin{algorithm}[Initial-support membership]
Given a vector $a=(a_0,\ldots,a_{L-1})$ and $n\leq L$, accept membership in the truncated $S_n$ exactly when $a_k=0$ for every $n\leq k<L$. The algorithm takes $O(L-n)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space. The unit vector $e_n$ certifies strictness between rungs $n$ and $n+1$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Power-divisibility membership]
Given sampled values $f(z_1),\ldots,f(z_L)$ and $n$, check whether each is congruent to zero modulo $2^n$. This uses $O(L)$ divisibility tests and $O(1)$ auxiliary space. The constant sample $2^n$ separates levels $n$ and $n+1$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Chain-orientation audit]
Represent finite approximations to $A_0,\ldots,A_m$ as bit sets over a universe of size $U$. Compare every adjacent pair. Report ascending if $A_i\subseteq A_{i+1}$ throughout, descending if $A_{i+1}\subseteq A_i$ throughout, and mixed otherwise; retain elements of set differences as strictness witnesses. The time complexity is $O(mU)$ and working space is $O(U)$.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Applications and interpretation}
In symbolic computation, Noetherianity supports finite generation and termination arguments. The countable-variable staircase pinpoints the obstruction: every finite variable prefix omits an independent next generator. In product systems, initial-support ideals model perturbations confined to finite coordinate windows, and each new coordinate enlarges the possible perturbations.

The divisibility example supplies a general constraint-audit pattern. If level $n+1$ imposes all requirements of level $n$ and more, the family is antitone. A boundary object satisfying level $n$ but not level $n+1$ proves strictness. This pattern applies to congruence depth, orders of vanishing, and filtration levels.

A proposed invariant must also be tested against known notions. Since staircase existence is equivalent to non-Noetherianity, a binary value merely renames an established property. A finite-variable count cannot be its height while the defining infinite staircase is absent.

\section{Toward a meaningful height}
A refined theory could take several forms. One may study the supremum of ordinal order types of strict ideal chains, subject to suitable size bounds. One may restrict attention to prime, radical, principal, or finitely generated ideals. Prime chains lead toward Krull dimension; principal chains reflect divisibility and factorization. Alternatively, a generator-growth profile could measure how many independent generators successive stages require. A computational version might count strict enlargements visible under a resource bound, though invariance under presentation would then require proof.

Any useful definition should be invariant under ring isomorphism, agree with intended examples, differ demonstrably from Noetherianity and Krull dimension, and be computable or estimable on a substantial family of rings.

\section{Algebraic integers}
The ring of all algebraic integers should be specified, for example, as the integral closure $\overline{\mathbb Z}$ of $\mathbb Z$ in a fixed algebraic closure $\overline{\mathbb Q}$. A promising non-Noetherianity argument chooses compatible roots of a prime. If $\alpha_{n+1}^{q}=\alpha_n$, then
\[
 (\alpha_n)\subseteq(\alpha_{n+1}).
\]
Strictness needs a further valuation or norm argument in finite subextensions. Once non-Noetherianity is proved, the characterization theorem supplies a staircase. It does not supply a canonical finite or ordinal height; that requires a coherent definition independent of choices of roots and ambient closure.

\section{Discussion and conclusion}
The existence problem is completely controlled by Noetherianity. Integer sequences and countably many polynomial variables give explicit positive examples, each adding one independent coordinate or generator at every stage. Finite-variable polynomial rings and $p$-adic integers are negative examples because the ascending chain condition holds.

Two conceptual corrections are essential. First, the intersection of an ascending chain indexed from a least member is automatically that member, and an ideal is not itself a ring element in the intersection. Second, higher divisibility exponents define smaller ideals: the pointwise $2^n$ construction descends strictly, with constant functions providing witnesses.

The Escher metaphor therefore has a precise interpretation. The endless climb is failure of the ascending chain condition; the common foundation is simply the least rung. A genuinely new height theory must add structure beyond existence, an automatic intersection identity, or Krull dimension. With those distinctions in place, the impossible architecture becomes a clear map of infinite ideal growth.

\end{document}

